title: Waves & Space
subject: Physics
level: GCSE
emoji: 🌌
description: Wave properties, the EM spectrum, light, sound and the universe.
---
What is the wave speed equation? :: Wave speed = frequency × wavelength (v = fλ)
What is frequency? :: The number of waves passing a point each second, in hertz (Hz).
What is a transverse wave? :: Oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer (e.g. light).
What is a longitudinal wave? :: Oscillations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer (e.g. sound).
Order the electromagnetic spectrum from low to high frequency. :: Radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma.
Which EM waves are used for cooking and satellite communication? :: Microwaves.
Which EM waves are used in remote controls and thermal imaging? :: Infrared.
Which EM waves can cause skin cancer? :: Ultraviolet.
Which EM waves are used to see bones? :: X-rays.
Which EM waves are used to sterilise medical equipment and treat cancer? :: Gamma rays.
How fast do all EM waves travel in a vacuum? :: 3 × 10⁸ m/s.
What is the law of reflection? :: Angle of incidence = angle of reflection.
What is refraction? :: A change in direction when a wave changes speed entering a different medium.
What type of lens is thicker in the middle? :: Convex (converging) lens.
What is the human hearing range? :: About 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz.
What is ultrasound used for? :: Medical imaging (e.g. scans of a baby) and cleaning.
What is the Big Bang theory? :: The universe began from a very small, hot, dense point and has been expanding ever since.
What is red-shift? :: Light from distant galaxies is shifted to longer wavelengths, showing they are moving away.
What is a light-year? :: The distance light travels in one year (≈ 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m).
What force keeps planets in orbit around the Sun? :: Gravity.
What are the stages of a star like our Sun? :: Nebula → protostar → main sequence → red giant → white dwarf.
What happens to a star much bigger than the Sun at the end of its life? :: Red supergiant → supernova → neutron star or black hole.
